w?\n\nWe send a real MCP handshake every 15 minutes. Over the last 24 hours 91 of 91 checks got a reply (100.0%), average response time 434 ms. The bar chart above shows every period we have measured.\n\nHow do I connect MCP security checks — WARDEN and HISTOR?\n\nCopy the ready config from this page. We generate it for Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex, Cursor and VS Code, each in the form that client actually reads. It is a remote server, so there is nothing to install — the client connects to the address.\n\nDoes MCP security checks — WARDEN and HISTOR need an API key?\n\nNo. MCP security checks — WARDEN and HISTOR completed a full MCP handshake with us as an anonymous client and listed its tools without asking for anything. All 4 of them are readable on this page. This is what we observed, not what the docs claim.\n\nHow fast is MCP security checks — WARDEN and HISTOR?\n\nIt answers our handshake in 434 ms on average, which is faster than 30% of all working MCP servers we measure. The comparison comes from our own checks across the whole registry, every 15 minutes.\n\nIs MCP security checks — WARDEN and HISTOR open source?\n\nThe code is open source: it is published under the MIT licence, written in TypeScript and 0 stars on GitHub. The licence covers the code in the repository, not the hosted service at modelmarket.dev, which runs on its operator's own terms.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWARDEN — MCP Security Firewall - Odel Module Marketplace (https://odel.app/modules/alexar76/warden)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; npm@aimarket/warden v0.16.0\n\nMarket/AI agent governance/WARDEN — MCP Security Firewall\n\nImage: WARDEN — MCP Security FirewallMCP server: One MCP server.\n\n# WARDEN — MCP Security Firewall\n\n@alexar76MCP server · External\n\nMCP security firewall: stdio wrap proxy or inspection tools; zero runtime dependencies.\n\nAI agent governancev0.16.0 MIT TypeScript\n\nFree no